{"type":"auditAdvisory","data":{"resolution":{"id":1243857,"path":"query-string>decode-uri-component","dev":false,"optional":false,"bundled":false},"advisory":{"findings":[{"version":"0.2.2","paths":["query-string>decode-uri-component","react-admin>ra-core>query-string>decode-uri-component","react-admin>ra-i18n-polyglot>ra-core>query-string>decode-uri-component"]}],"found_by":null,"deleted":null,"references":"- https://github.com/SamVerschueren/decode-uri-component/security/advisories/GHSA-qr28-fpjm-wq7f\n- https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-118664\n- https://github.com/SamVerschueren/decode-uri-component/commit/fad01d65b93bd3f2f0dc32dfd8d57a38b57bc608\n- https://github.com/SamVerschueren/decode-uri-component/releases/tag/v0.5.1\n- https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-qr28-fpjm-wq7f","created":"2026-10-29T16:41:28.000Z","id":1243857,"npm_advisory_id":null,"overview":"### Summary\n\n`decodeUriComponent()` takes quadratic time on malformed input that has many distinct percent-encoded runs. A single unauthenticated request of about 224 KB can block the Node.js event loop for about 20 seconds. Version 0.5.0, the release that fixed CVE-2026-45822, is affected.\n\n### Details\n\nWhen native decodeURIComponent throws, `decodeUriComponent()` falls back to `customDecodeURIComponent` (`index.js:122`). That function:\n1. Scans the input for every run of percent-encoded bytes (e.g. `%C3%A5`) and stores each distinct run, with its decoded value, in a lookup table (`index.js:130-144`).\n2. Runs one full-string `input.replace(new RegExp(key, 'g'), …)` per table entry (`index.js:154`).\n\nWith K distinct runs in an input of length L, that's K regex compilations and K full scans of the string, so O(K·L). An attacker controls both K and L, and K grows with L. One malformed sequence such as a trailing `%zz` is enough to force the fallback path.\n\nThe 0.5.0 fix for CVE-2026-45822 (`fa479da`) replaced the recursive `decode()` routine with a linear scanner, but left this replacement loop unchanged.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nRunning the following code takes ~20 seconds.\n\n```js\nimport decodeUriComponent from 'decode-uri-component';\n\nlet payload = '';\nfor (let i = 0; i < 32000; i++) {\n\tconst high = Math.floor(i / 256).toString(16).padStart(2, '0');\n\tconst low = (i % 256).toString(16).padStart(2, '0');\n\tpayload += `%${high}%${low}x`; // distinct run each iteration\n}\npayload += '%zz'; // forces the fallback decoder\n\nconsole.time('decode');\ndecodeUriComponent(payload); // ~224 KB\nconsole.timeEnd('decode');\n```\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is an availability issue only and there's no confidentiality or integrity impact. Any application that passes untrusted input to `decodeUriComponent()`, directly or through a dependency such as `query-string`, can be made unresponsive. Because Node.js runs on one thread, one request stalls every other client.\n\nThe worst case needs input well beyond URL length limits. Node's default 16 KB header limit keeps payloads passed in via a URL query string to roughly 40 ms per request. Applications that decode request bodies are fully exposed, for example form bodies parsed with `query-string.parse()`.","reported_by":null,"title":"decode-uri-component vulnerable to denial of service via quadratic replacement of percent-encoded runs","metadata":null,"cves":["CVE-2026-118664"],"access":"public","severity":"high","module_name":"decode-uri-component","vulnerable_versions":"<=0.5.0","github_advisory_id":"GHSA-qr28-fpjm-wq7f","recommendation":"Upgrade to version 0.5.1 or later","patched_versions":">=0.5.1","updated":"2026-10-29T16:41:29.000Z","cvss":{"score":0,"vectorString":null},"cwe":["CWE-400","CWE-407"],"url":"https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-qr28-fpjm-wq7f"}}}
{"type":"auditAdvisory","data":{"resolution":{"id":1244012,"path":"ejs>jake>filelist>minimatch","dev":false,"optional":false,"bundled":false},"advisory":{"findings":[{"version":"10.2.6","paths":["ejs>jake>filelist>minimatch"]}],"found_by":null,"deleted":null,"references":"- https://github.com/isaacs/minimatch/security/advisories/GHSA-g3hv-75wg-m249\n- https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-118764\n- https://github.com/isaacs/minimatch/commit/77a0f5c4b80bdc641c3c930a1d9edf5ad2db57f8\n- https://github.com/isaacs/minimatch/releases/tag/v11.0.1\n- https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-g3hv-75wg-m249","created":"2026-10-30T23:09:55.000Z","id":1244012,"npm_advisory_id":null,"overview":"minimatch 10.2.6 contains an attacker-controlled pattern amplification in firstPhasePreProcess() when optimizationLevel >= 2.\n\nresearcher: Reid Downing (b1nv0id)\nfiles: \n\n- src/index.ts: 690-770\n- src/index.ts: 780-796\n\nissue:\ninput-controlled growth of globParts during first-phase preprocessing, followed by quadratic second-phase processing.\n\nthe **/../ transformation clones a globparts entry and appends the clone to the same globparts array:\n```\nparts.splice(gs, 1)\nconst other = parts.slice(0)\nother[gs] = '**'\nglobParts.push(other)\n```\nthis occurs inside a do...while (didSomething) fixpoint loop, allowing generated entries to be processed again.\n\nthe expanded globparts array is subsequently processed by secondPhasePreProcess(), which performs pairwise comparisons:\n```\nfor (let i = 0; i < globParts.length - 1; i++) {\n  for (let j = i + 1; j < globParts.length; j++) {\n    this.partsMatch(...)\n  }\n}\n```\nan attacker who can supply a glob pattern can therefore cause additional pattern entries to be generated during preprocessing, followed by quadratic pairwise processing, resulting in excessive cpu and memory consumption.","reported_by":null,"title":"minimatch has potential denial of service via globparts amplification","metadata":null,"cves":["CVE-2026-118764"],"access":"public","severity":"moderate","module_name":"minimatch","vulnerable_versions":"<11.0.1","github_advisory_id":"GHSA-g3hv-75wg-m249","recommendation":"Upgrade to version 11.0.1 or later","patched_versions":">=11.0.1","updated":"2026-10-30T23:09:56.000Z","cvss":{"score":0,"vectorString":null},"cwe":["CWE-405"],"url":"https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-g3hv-75wg-m249"}}}
{"type":"auditAdvisory","data":{"resolution":{"id":1244003,"path":"follow-redirects","dev":false,"optional":false,"bundled":false},"advisory":{"findings":[{"version":"1.16.1","paths":["follow-redirects"]}],"found_by":null,"deleted":null,"references":"- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-mrwp-f3q8-3g9p\n- https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-116689\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/b0a7751e9cd64cd8eeb6ec67d2f206a9cc88d3f8\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/releases/tag/v1.16.2\n- https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-mrwp-f3q8-3g9p","created":"2026-10-30T22:59:33.000Z","id":1244003,"npm_advisory_id":null,"overview":"### Summary\n\nfollow-redirects 1.16.0 and 1.16.1 validate the documented sensitiveHeaders option with instanceof Array. An array created in another JavaScript realm is silently replaced with an empty list, so a caller-declared custom credential header can be forwarded to an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect destination.\n\n### Details\n\nAffected versions\n\nThe sensitiveHeaders option was introduced in 1.16.0. Published 1.16.0 and 1.16.1 both reproduced, giving a tested affected range of 1.16.0 through 1.16.1. The source checkout was pinned at revision 46301256333c90b7faac7aa126effe72ec16d16e; the exact npm 1.16.1 artifact reproduced independently.\n\nRoot cause\n\nindex.js resets options.sensitiveHeaders when its local isArray() helper returns false. That helper uses value instanceof Array, which depends on realm identity. The same file already uses Array.isArray(options.headers), which correctly recognizes arrays across realms.\n\nSuggested remediation\n\nUse Array.isArray(value) for sensitiveHeaders, then validate and normalize each member as a header-name string. Add same-realm and node:vm cross-realm tests for cross-origin redirects, including the array-form headers representation. Verify that custom sensitive names are removed while unrelated headers remain unchanged.\n\nDuplicate review\n\nThe public advisory history contains other sensitive-header findings, including array-form headers and regular-expression handling, but focused repository searches found no report describing cross-realm validation as of 2026-10-25. A private or differently worded report may already exist.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nStart two loopback HTTP servers. The first returns a 302 redirect to the second. Send X-API-Key and list that name in sensitiveHeaders, then compare a normal array with a genuine cross-realm array created by:\n\n```js\nconst vm = require('node:vm');\nconst sensitiveHeaders = vm.runInNewContext(`['X-API-Key']`);\n```\n\nObserved against both source and the exact npm 1.16.1 package:\n\n- Same-realm ['X-API-Key']: the redirect target did not receive the key.\n- Cross-realm ['X-API-Key']: Array.isArray(value) was true, value instanceof Array was false, and the redirect target received X-API-Key.\n- An unrelated X-Harmless header remained present in both cases.\n\nThe complete 3.5 KB loopback-only harness and raw JSON output are available privately.\n\n### Impact\n\ncustom credential disclosure. Exploitation requires a direct caller or wrapper that preserves a cross-realm sensitiveHeaders array, a custom credential listed in that option, and an attacker-controlled or compromised endpoint that returns a cross-origin redirect. The directly demonstrated result is disclosure of the custom header value, potentially exposing an API key or service token until revocation.\n\nBuilt-in Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, and Cookie protections remained effective. Exact Axios 1.20.0 with follow-redirects 1.16.1 did not reproduce because Axios normalized the array; this report does not claim that Axios is affected. No third-party service was tested.","reported_by":null,"title":"follow-redirects: Cross-realm sensitiveHeaders arrays are discarded and custom credentials can follow redirects","metadata":null,"cves":["CVE-2026-116689"],"access":"public","severity":"high","module_name":"follow-redirects","vulnerable_versions":">=1.16.0 <=1.16.1","github_advisory_id":"GHSA-mrwp-f3q8-3g9p","recommendation":"Upgrade to version 1.16.2 or later","patched_versions":">=1.16.2","updated":"2026-10-30T22:59:34.000Z","cvss":{"score":5.9,"vectorString":"CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N"},"cwe":["CWE-201"],"url":"https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-mrwp-f3q8-3g9p"}}}
